be looking at  restrictions on indoor mixing this  christmas can families be reassured that  that is not going to happen now well as  the prime minister set out yesterday we  keep these issues under close review  obviously there's considerable  uncertainty within the omicron data were  concerned about the increasing growth in  cases particularly in london but there  is a considerable time lag between those  catching the the infection and  presenting as cases at hospital and of  course that needs to look in the round  alongside the booster campaign the  increase in treatments  the changes in behavior as a consequence  of plan b  which will also have an impact on those  hospital admissions so that's why we're  looking so closely at the hospital data  particularly around london where omicron  is more prevalent  and we're balancing that against the  wider economic consequences of any  further restrictions which also carry  health risks for reasons tomorrow you've  often highlighted on the program in  terms of the mental health consequences  for example as well as those wider  economic consequences of course hospital  data is hugely important are you  expecting any more data this week is  there a critical point coming up where  you think that decision will need to be  made well we're getting data all the  time on an hour by hour day by day basis  both internationally we're looking at  data although there are issues around  the comparability of that people often  will say for example look at the south  african data we need to be conscious  that the age profile is different it's  summer there as opposed to winter here  their health care system is different so  we're looking internationally at data  whether in south africa whether in  denmark or elsewhere we're looking  regionally at the different profiles for  example london uh where over 88 percent  of cases um uh presenting as emissions  are macron compared to 43 44 in the  north east so we're looking at regional  data w